declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<h2>Five Sites of Enslavement</h2>';

echo '<h3>1. The Aro Network: Religion as Slave Trade Infrastructure</h3>';
echo '<p>The Aro Chukwu oracle at Arochukwu was the most important religious and judicial institution in precolonial Igboland. The Aro people — the oracle\'s custodians and the most far-ranging traders in Igboland — built a commercial network that extended across the entire region and beyond. In the 18th and 19th centuries, this network became the primary infrastructure of the Atlantic slave trade in the Bight of Biafra.</p>';
echo '<p>The mechanism was sophisticated. Communities across Igboland brought disputes to Arochukwu for resolution — land cases, matrimonial disputes, accusations of witchcraft. The oracle\'s priests — who were Aro traders simultaneously — could pronounce a judgment that effectively "consumed" the guilty party: the individual disappeared into the oracle\'s precincts and emerged at the coast, enslaved, to be sold to European traders at Calabar, Bonny, or Brass. The religious authority of the oracle made the mechanism almost impossible to resist: to contest an oracle judgment was itself impiety.</p>';
echo '<p>Aro long-distance traders also moved through Igboland with armed escorts, purchasing captives from wars and raids, and conducting their own kidnapping operations. The Aro were not the only enslaving group in Igboland — many communities participated in the trade — but they were its organising centre. The British Aro Expedition of 1901-1902, which destroyed the oracle, was explicitly aimed at dismantling this infrastructure. It required 87 officers, 3,161 men, and naval support — not a police action but a war against a resistant and organised trading state.</p>';

echo '<h3>2. Igbo Landing: Resistance Through Death</h3>';
echo '<p>On the morning of 8 June 1803, a slave ship arrived at Dunbar Creek on St. Simons Island, Georgia, carrying a group of Igbo captives. What happened next has been told and retold across two centuries: the Igbo captives, still chained together, overturned the longboat taking them ashore, drowned their captors, and — led by their chief — walked into the waters of Dunbar Creek singing, choosing death over enslavement. The Igbo phrase associated with the event is <em>Ebo e bụ mmiri, ebo e laa n\'mmiri</em> — "The Ibo are water people; the Ibo return to water."</p>';
echo '<p>The historical record of the event is fragmentary — it comes primarily from insurance records (the enslaved represented a financial loss) and from oral tradition. Historians have debated its precise details. What is not debated is its cultural significance: Igbo Landing became one of the most powerful stories of enslaved resistance in American history, preserving in the oral tradition of Gullah communities for two centuries before being recovered and commemorated. It has inspired novels (Toni Morrison\'s <em>Song of Solomon</em> draws on the tradition), plays, films, and an annual commemoration at the site. It represents something true about Igbo responses to enslavement regardless of its precise historical accuracy: that resistance was a dimension of the Igbo experience in the Atlantic trade from the beginning.</p>';

echo '<h3>3. The Bight of Biafra: Geography of the Trade</h3>';
echo '<p>The Bight of Biafra was exceptional in the Atlantic slave trade for the high proportion of Igbo among those exported and for the relatively late intensification of the trade — the Bight did not become a major exporting region until the mid-17th century, but by the 18th century it was one of the most active in the entire Atlantic system. The major ports — Calabar (Cross River), Bonny (Rivers State), and Brass (Bayelsa State) — were controlled by non-Igbo coastal peoples: the Efik at Calabar, the Ijaw at Bonny, the Nembe at Brass. These communities became the commercial intermediaries between the Aro inland trading network and the European slave ships anchored offshore.</p>';
echo '<p>The Efik of Calabar developed particularly sophisticated commercial relations with British traders. The Old Calabar trading houses — names like Ephraim Robin John and Grandy King George — appear in British trading records as business partners, correspondents, and, in some documented cases, as individuals who sent their sons to be educated in England. This commercial relationship did not make the trade less brutal; it made it more systematically organised. The Efik grew wealthy from the trade. The Igbo interior communities from which the captives came were devastated.</p>';

echo '<h3>4. Abolition: Who Did the Work</h3>';
echo '<p>The abolition of the slave trade is often narrated as a European moral achievement — the triumph of Enlightenment values over commercial interest. This narrative erases the people who actually did the work of abolition. The Haitian Revolution (1791-1804) — in which enslaved people overthrew the most profitable slave colony in the world, defeated the armies of France, Britain, and Spain, and founded the first Black republic — was the single most powerful argument against slavery that the 18th century produced. It demonstrated that enslaved people were human beings capable of political organisation, military strategy, and self-governance. It terrified slaveholders across the Americas and accelerated the British calculation that the trade was becoming too dangerous to maintain.</p>';
echo '<p>Olaudah Equiano — Igbo-born, formerly enslaved, settled in Britain — was the most important Black voice in the British abolitionist movement. His <em>Interesting Narrative</em> (1789) was submitted to Parliament, sold thousands of copies, and put an Igbo face and an Igbo voice on the abstract moral argument that Enlightenment philosophers were making. Frederick Douglass did the same in the United States a generation later. The white abolitionist movement — Wilberforce, Clarkson, the Quakers — provided political infrastructure, legal strategy, and parliamentary leverage. But the moral urgency came from the enslaved themselves.</p>';

echo '<h3>5. The Osu System: Internal Exclusion and Contemporary Consequences</h3>';
echo '<p>The osu system is one of the most difficult and contested aspects of Igbo social life. The <em>osu</em> — individuals dedicated to a deity and their descendants — are permanently excluded from certain forms of social participation: they cannot marry free-born (<em>diala</em>) Igbo, cannot hold certain traditional titles, and were historically excluded from full community membership. The system predates the Atlantic trade. It is not a product of colonialism. It is an internal Igbo social institution with ancient religious roots.</p>';
echo '<p>Its contemporary consequences are real and harmful. Young Igbo people — in Nigeria and in diaspora communities — report that osu status continues to affect marriage negotiations, that families conduct genealogical investigations to establish freedom from osu descent before approving marriages, and that the stigma is transmitted across generations even among people who have never lived in Igboland and may not speak Igbo. The Eastern Nigerian Osu Abolition Law of 1956 formally prohibited osu discrimination; the law has not abolished the practice.</p>';
echo '<p>Chinua Achebe addressed the osu system directly in <em>No Longer at Ease</em> (1960) — a novel in which an Igbo man\'s family forbids his marriage to a woman of osu descent, destroying both of them in the process. By placing this conflict at the centre of a novel that reached international audiences, Achebe brought internal Igbo social hierarchies to global attention and opened a debate that continues. The osu system raises profound questions about inherited identity, religious tradition, and social justice that Igbo communities — in Nigeria and in diaspora — are actively working through.</p>';
echo '</div>';